Original commit body from 3a03c65435799b180ca735ce3f79fdc2611b943d:

The recorded rust-analyzer fixture carried upstream aardvark-dns's
Cargo.lock, and its pins drew five Dependabot alerts on the default
branch: hickory-proto 0.25.2 (GHSA-3v94-mw7p-v465, which has no patched
release, and GHSA-q2qq-hmj6-3wpp), bytes 1.10.1 (GHSA-434x-w66g-qw3r),
time 0.3.44 (GHSA-r6v5-fh4h-64xc) and rand 0.9.2 (GHSA-cq8v-f236-94qc).
Nothing here builds the crate. The fixture tests run the tree-sitter
Rust analyzer over the .rs files only, discovery excludes Cargo.lock as
a generated lock file, and the wheel never shipped recorded/. Bumping
the pins could not clear the hickory-proto advisory and would have
desynced the lock from the recorded index.

The lock still matters for one thing: index.scip's external monikers
name the versions it resolved (hickory-proto 0.25.2, tokio 1.49.0, ...),
so a re-record without it would change them. Upstream's Cargo.lock at
4444d90fee is byte-identical to the deleted one, so the README now gives
its URL and SHA-256 and the re-record steps restore it, then delete it
again. The README commands were run once and the hash check passes.

All 13 test files that reference the fixture pass without the file (422
tests); they are added to the CI manifest because the slicer selects
none of them for a lockfile-only change.

